      {
        "id": 116030,
        "name": "Ronway",
        "username": "Ronway",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ronway/{size}/1856_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-08-23T01:14:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"lutherbellhendricksv\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/lutherbellhendricksv/48/2350_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> lutherbellhendricksv:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>Ronway wrote:</b><br><blockquote>&nbsp;SeraAlexandria wrote:<br><blockquote>since you can't replace the card you pick up, would have to wait a turn after their incapacitation to replay them.&nbsp;&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nActually, since Citizens have their own turn, this will allow them to stand up from Incapacitation on their turn, rather than having to take the card back and playing it at another time. However you would still need to do so if you want to switch Citizens.\n</blockquote></div>\nOnly characters remain on the board when incapacitated. Note the Citizen Dawn scenarios that specify that the Citizens in those scenarios break this rule.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Oh wow, I guess there was a rule I was mis playing. Fortunately I don't think I played Dawn during playtesting. So woo!</p>\n<p>Edit: Though I did play her first scenario a crap ton of times. I guess that's probably why I thought Citizens stood back up from incap, rather than it was exclusive to Scenarios.</p>",
